# Cloudflare Pages response headers. Copied verbatim into build/ by SvelteKit.
#
# No COOP/COEP here — because nothing needs cross-origin isolation, *not*
# because it would break anything.
#
# This corrects what this comment used to claim (2026-08-29). The old note said
# isolation would block the Kokoro model-mirror fetches. Measured, it does not:
# under COOP: same-origin + COEP: require-corp the app boots with
# crossOriginIsolated = true and both sherpa artifacts still load 200. COEP's
# CORP requirement applies to *no-cors* subresources only, and
# `static/tts/sherpa-worker.js` uses a bare `fetch(url)` — mode `cors` — against
# a mirror that sends `access-control-allow-origin: *`, which COEP permits.
#
# So isolation is an open door rather than a closed one, should anything ever
# want SharedArrayBuffer. Nothing does today. Verified in Firefox only; check
# Chrome before actually enabling it.
#
# One thing has since walked through that door and would be shut out by it
# (2026-08-30): the reader's follow view embeds YouTube's iframe player, and a
# cross-origin frame is blocked under COEP: require-corp unless it sends CORP or
# COEP of its own, which YouTube does not. Nothing here forbids isolation, but
# enabling it now costs the YouTube reader.
#
# And no CSP is set — deliberately, and this is the record of it having been
# checked: the follow view loads https://www.youtube.com/iframe_api and frames
# https://www.youtube-nocookie.com. Adding a policy means naming both in
# script-src and frame-src, or the player fails with no visible error.

/_app/immutable/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
